TOPOLOGY AS TESTED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  One port carries four clients.  Three of them authenticate, each by a different
  method.  The fourth is the control and qualifies for none of them.  The DUT is both
  the authenticator and the RADIUS server, so no second switch and no external server
  took part.

        tb277  eth4  00:0d:b9:55:77:0f  10.38.22.65/27
             |
             |  MAC X 02:0d:b9:00:42:79, 10.38.22.73  -> MAC authentication
             |  MAC Y 02:0d:b9:00:42:7a               -> 802.1X as ckmulti1
             |  MAC Z 02:0d:b9:00:42:7b, 10.38.22.75  -> the CONTROL, bad credentials
             |  the NIC's own MAC and address         -> web authentication as ckweb
             |
             |  (down)  EAPOL frames, ICMP echo requests, and one HTTP POST
             |  (up)    EAP frames, ICMP echo replies when authorised, HTTP responses
             v
    +--------------------------------------------------------+
    |  swi_d   AT-x960-56YSQ                       <-- DUT   |
    |  port1.0.48: auth-mac enable,                          |
    |              auth-web enable,                          |
    |              dot1x port-control auto,                  |
    |              auth host-mode multi-supplicant,          |
    |              auth forward arp / dhcp / dns             |
    |                                                        |
    |  auth-web-server ipaddress 10.38.22.66, HTTP port 80   |
    |         |                                              |
    |         +--> radius-server local, 127.0.0.1:1812       |
    |                users 02-0d-b9-00-42-79 and ckweb       |
    |                added for this case, plus ckmulti1      |
    +--------------------------------------------------------+
             ^
             |  console, 115200 baud
        tb277  /dev/u2

  DUT     : swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) console u2 at 115200 baud, S/N A2WQS700B,
            software sin_x96056-20261019-27.  Not a stack.  vlan 48 address
            10.38.22.66/27, MAC e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43.
  server  : the DUT's own local RADIUS server.  `radius-server local`, NAS 127.0.0.1.
            This case added the local users `02-0d-b9-00-42-79` (the MAC credential)
            and `ckweb` (the web credential), and the method lists
            `aaa authentication auth-mac default group radius` and
            `aaa authentication auth-web default group radius`.  All were removed at
            the end.
  link    : tb277 eth4 <-> swi_d port1.0.48 (access port in vlan 48)
  probe   : the dataplane probe in this case is an ICMP echo request to 10.38.22.66,
            not an ARP request.  See the note below.
  tools   : tools/eapol.py (802.1X), tools/arpleg.py with `--icmp` (the dataplane
            probe), tools/httpq.py (the web login POST), tools/tb ping, tools/tb ckcon
            for the console, tools/qmark.py for the read-only help probe, and
            tools/tb health for the two health reads

  Why the probe is ICMP and not ARP.  `auth-web enable` adds `auth forward arp`,
  `auth forward dhcp` and `auth forward dns` to the port by itself, and they are
  visible in the running-config.  Web authentication cannot work without them: a client
  that has not logged in yet must be able to ARP for the gateway, get an address and
  resolve a name before it can reach a login page.  With `auth forward arp` in force,
  ARP is forwarded for clients that have not authenticated, so an ARP probe can no
  longer tell the two states apart.  Every dataplane measurement in this case therefore
  uses ICMP echo, which is not in the forward list and is blocked until the client is
  authorised.  `arpleg.py --icmp` was run with `--gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43` and
  `--answer`, so the DUT can resolve the sender.  The numbers in this log come from a
  different probe than the ARP counts used elsewhere.

  Second rig fact: the DUT tries MAC authentication for every new MAC before it tries
  802.1X.  STEP 2 measures what that costs.

