STEP 2 -- method two: 802.1X, with its control in the SAME packet window     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: both clients were put in one `eapol.py` run, so the refusal and the
  acceptance share one window.

pcap -> /tmp/authm1304/T44279-S2.pcap (12 EAPOL frames)
     3.028 02:0d:b9:00:42:7a <- EAP-Success id=2    expected success, got success -- MATCH
     3.032 02:0d:b9:00:42:7b <- EAP-Failure id=2    expected failure, got failure -- MATCH
RESULT T44279-S2-dot1x-plus-CONTROL: 2/2 supplicant(s) matched their expectation -- MATCH

LEG T44279-S2-dot1x-dataplane      (MAC Y)  cap 10 frames: x4 echo request, x4 echo REPLY
LEG T44279-S2-CONTROL-dataplane    (MAC Z)  cap  4 frames: x4 echo request, 0 replies

  Why EAP-Success arrives at 3.028 s here, where other 802.1X legs on this bench land
  at 1.02 to 1.05 s.  The DUT's own log gives the reason:

  radiusd: (2) Login incorrect (No Auth-Type found: rejecting the user via
           Post-Auth-Type = Reject): [02-0d-b9-00-42-7a]

  With `auth-mac enable` on the port the DUT tries MAC authentication first for every
  new MAC.  MAC Y has no MAC user, so that attempt is rejected, and only then does the
  802.1X exchange run.  The extra 2 seconds is the cost of that first attempt, not a
  slow server.  The same line appears for MAC Z.

  => STEP 2 PASS: the second method works.  802.1X admitted MAC Y with 4 echo replies
     out of 4 and refused MAC Z with an explicit EAP-Failure and 0 out of 4, in the same
     window.  The MAC-authenticated client from STEP 1 kept its session throughout.

STEP 3 -- method three: web authentication                                   PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: interception was measured first.  A plain GET from the unauthenticated host
  returns the DUT's own page:

host# GET http://10.38.22.66/   -> HTTP 200 OK, body contains:
<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="3;URL=http://10.38.22.66/index.cgi?authenticating=1">

  That is the web authentication server answering, not the management HTTP server,
  which on this unit answers `/` with 404.

  The login page at `index.cgi?authenticating=1` holds exactly this:

<FORM action="http://10.38.22.66/index.cgi" autocomplete="off" target="_self" name="AUTH" method="POST">
<INPUT size="30" type="text"     maxlength="64" name="USERNAME" tabindex="0">
<INPUT size="30" type="password" maxlength="64" name="PASSWORD" tabindex="0">
<INPUT class="btncolor" type="submit" name="ACTION" value="login" tabindex="0">

  There is no cookie, no session token, no CSRF token and no JavaScript in the submit
  path.  So the whole login protocol is one form POST, and `tools/httpq.py` drove it
  without any change.  No HTTP client had to be written.

  The control was run FIRST, so that it cannot be an effect of an earlier success:

host# POST http://10.38.22.66/index.cgi
        USERNAME=ckweb&PASSWORD=WRONGPASSWORD9&ACTION=login
  HTTP 200 OK -- the body refreshes back to index.cgi?authenticating=1 (the login page)

