STEP 5 -- removal of the web limb                                            PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# interface port1.0.48
awplus(config-if)# no auth-web enable
awplus(config-if)# end

  | check after `no auth-web enable`      | result                                              |
  | ------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
  | show auth supplicant ... brief         | totalSupplicantNum: 0 -- every session on the port cleared |
  | host eth4 ping 10.38.22.66             | 0 of 4 (was 4 of 4)                                 |
  | GET http://10.38.22.66/                | TRANSPORT FAILED: <urlopen error timed out>         |

  One reporting inconsistency was measured.  Straight after `no auth-web enable`, with
  the port already behaving as above, the global summary still read:

awplus# show auth
WEB-based Port Authentication   : Enabled

  It only changed to `Disabled` once `no auth-web-server ipaddress` was also removed.

  => STEP 5 PASS: the removal is real at three levels at once.  The sessions are gone,
     the dataplane is blocked again, and the intercepting server no longer answers.
     The global `show auth` WEB line follows the global server configuration, not
     whether any port still has `auth-web enable`.  It is not wrong about the server,
     but it is a poor way to read a port's state.  Anyone who read only `show auth`
     after disabling web authentication on the last port would think the feature was
     still active there.  This is why the case was graded on the wire and on the
     per-client table.

STEP 6 -- two observations recorded, neither changing the verdict            done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. A command that worked, and logged an internal error about it.

  Two `user.err` lines appeared at 03:37:14 UTC:

  IMI[953]: AAA: failed to update method-list default (unknown type 7).

  That is exactly when the teardown ran `no aaa authentication auth-mac default` and
  `no aaa authentication auth-web default`: two errors for two commands.  Both commands
  took effect.  `show running-config | include aaa` afterwards holds only the three
  baseline lines, and both method lists are gone.  So the operation succeeded and the
  device logged an internal error that the operator never sees at the command line.
  Nothing was printed on the console; the lines are only in `show log`.  The cause needs
  a developer.

  2. The local RADIUS daemon restarted during this case.

  `radiusd` ran as process 6457 earlier on this unit and as process 14569 in this
  case's log lines.  This is not a crash.  `show exception log` is unchanged from the
  state read before the case, there is no new core file and no reboot, and the server
  answered the MAC, 802.1X and web authentications of this case correctly throughout.
  The likely trigger is the local server being reconfigured when
  `aaa authentication auth-web default group radius` and `auth-web-server ipaddress`
  were added, because adding a user alone did not restart it.  Recorded because a
  process id change is cheap to note now and impossible to reconstruct later.

